[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114369,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Calcifying odontogenic cyst (COC) is a developmental odontogenic cyst characterized histologically by ghost cells, which often calcify.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114370,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114371,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA05.0 &amp; XH3R33 Developmental odontogenic cysts &amp; Calcifying odontogenic cyst</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114372,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> calcifying cystic odontogenic tumour; Gorlin cyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114373,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114374,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>COC usually arises in the tooth-bearing segments of the jaws, almost equally in the maxilla and mandible. In the maxilla there is a strong predilection for the anterior segments, whereas mandibular lesions are more evenly distributed {2005490; 18221328; 29738629}. Approximately 10% of cases are extraosseous with a predilection for the anterior mandibular gingiva {27669959}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114375,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most patients are asymptomatic, although intrabony lesions typically cause cortical expansion. Extraosseous lesions present as submucosal swellings {29738629; 33591801}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>COCs generally form well-defined unilocular lesions; about one&nbsp;third are uniformly radiolucent, and the remainder are of mixed radiolucency. Cortical expansion is common {29738629; 33591801}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114376,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>COC shows a worldwide distribution {29738629} but is rare, accounting for &lt;&nbsp;1% of odontogenic cysts {16918602}. There is no sex predilection, and the peak incidence is in the second and third decades of life.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114377,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114378,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "<p >COC has mutations in <em>CTNNB1</em> (which encodes &beta;-catenin), a feature shared with adamantinomatous craniopharyngioma and pilomatrixoma {10192393; 27158066; 30598409} and implicating the WNT pathway {14578169; 28658279}. No other pathogenic variants in oncogenes or tumour suppressor genes were detected in a 50-gene panel {27158066}. WNT is also involved in odontomas, with which COC is often associated. It is not yet known whether the mutations cause a replication error phenotype.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-10T16:52:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114379,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is a cystic cavity architecturally, unless the cyst is curetted.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114380,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>COC is a unilocular cyst with a stratified epithelial lining of varying thickness, often loose, resembling stellate reticulum, and with palisaded and hyperchromatic columnar ameloblast-like basal cells. Cysts may show intraluminal and/or mural epithelial proliferation producing ameloblastoma-like areas. Ghost cells are characteristic rounded or stacked flattened pale eosinophilic cells within the epithelium, with distinct outlines and karyolysis resulting in a ghost-like appearance. These are variable in morphology and number, and they often calcify. Masses of ghost cells often pass into the connective tissue of the cyst wall, eliciting a foreign body reaction and inducing dentinoid (dentine-like matrix or mineralized tissue without a tubular structure) {2005490; 18221328}. Although characteristic, ghost cells form in other odontogenic tumours and do not alone justify a diagnosis of COC. COCs can occur with odontomas or other mixed odontogenic tumours {15472661}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes dentinogenic ghost cell tumour {27669959} and ghost cell odontogenic carcinoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114381,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114382,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114383,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> cystic architecture; numerous ghost cells.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> palisaded hyperchromatic basal cells; dentinoid.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114384,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114385,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 130,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>COCs are treated by conservative surgical removal, enucleation, and/or curettage. A systematic review reported an 8% recurrence rate {29738629}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:20:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]